<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>{{title}}, for approval</title>
<style>
body{font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#111;margin:0;padding:0}
h1{font-size:18px;margin:0 0 12px}
h2{font-size:15px;margin:18px 0 8px}
p{margin:0 0 10px}
a{color:#111}
.doc{border-left:3px solid #999;padding:4px 0 4px 14px;margin:0 0 16px}
.ref{margin:0 0 12px}
.ref b{display:block}
.mono{font-family:"Courier New",monospace;font-size:12px;word-break:break-all;overflow-wrap:anywhere}
.sign{margin-top:32px}
.sign p{margin:0 0 34px}
.anchor{color:#ffffff;font-size:10px}
</style>
</head>
<body>
<h1>{{title}}, for approval</h1>
<p>Drift King Compliance presents this for approval. Its full text follows.</p>
<div class="doc">
{{#paragraphs}}<p>{{text}}{{#link}}<br><a class="mono" href="{{link}}">{{link}}</a>{{/link}}</p>
{{/paragraphs}}</div>
<h2>The files</h2>
<p>Each sha256 is the fingerprint of the file's exact bytes. If one character changes, the fingerprint changes and this approval no longer covers it.</p>
{{#rows}}<div class="ref"><b>{{label}}</b>
{{^access}}<a class="mono" href="{{url}}">{{url}}</a><br>
<span class="mono">sha256 {{sha256}}</span>{{/access}}{{access}}</div>
{{/rows}}<h2>What the checks found</h2>
<p>Drift King ran each check below on the files above, in the build that made this page. A canary is a copy broken on purpose: a check must find something in it first, or finding nothing in the real thing means nothing.</p>
{{#results}}<div class="ref"><b>{{check}}</b>
Ran on: {{on}}<br>
Found: {{found}}. {{verdict}}</div>
{{/results}}<div class="sign">
<p>Signed, Drift King Compliance: <span class="anchor">/sig1/</span></p>
<p>Date: <span class="anchor">/date1/</span></p>
<p>Approved: <span class="anchor">/sig2/</span></p>
<p>Date: <span class="anchor">/date2/</span></p>
</div>
</body>
</html>
